\chapter{Domain analysis}

\section{Concepts}

Many climbing knots are tied \emph{around} an anchor object or \emph{join}
two ropes. A rope alone cannot express them, so the domain has these
concepts.

\begin{description}
\item[Rope] A flexible curve of finite thickness, with two ends. It is the
thing the player moves.
\item[Anchor object] A fixed object the rope can be tied around: a bar, a
carabiner, a ring, or a tree. All anchor types are in the first version. Without
it the topology of a hitch is incomplete.
\item[Second rope] A second rope, for bends that join two ropes.
\item[Stopper end] The free end of a rope in a rappel setup. A stopper keeps the
rope from running through the device.
\item[Knot] One or more ropes together with their anchor objects, in a
configuration the game recognises. A rope and its anchors count as one knot.
\item[Stage] A point in the climbing process: anchoring, belaying, rappel,
cleaning, tie-in, or rescue. Each knot belongs to one or more stages.
\item[Lesson] A teaching unit: one knot, its stage, its guided steps, and its
quiz.
\end{description}

\section{Invariants}

An invariant is a law that, if broken, means the game is no longer about
climbing knots.

\begin{enumerate}
\item A rope cannot pass through itself or through an anchor object. A knot
changes only when the rope is moved through a crossing or around an object.
\item A knot is identified by its topology, with its anchor objects included.
Two configurations are the same knot when one can be dragged into the other
without passing through the rope or an anchor. The same rope around a bar and
around a carabiner can be different knots.
\item A bend joins two rope ends. Its ends must be the ends of the two ropes,
not points in the middle. Mixing a bend and a hitch is an error.
\item A stopper knot is valid only at a free rope end. A middle overhand is not
a stopper.
\item The rope keeps its length. Moving one part pulls the rest along.
\item A knot stays tied while nothing moves it. Only the player's input and the
minimal tension change the shape.
\item Friction hitches are recognised by shape only. The game does not model
how they grip under load.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Closure of open ends}

A rope has two free ends, but a topological knot is a closed curve. For
recognition, each free end is extended straight back, away from the viewer,
to a point behind the scene, and the two extensions are joined there. The
join is never drawn. Because it lies behind everything, any crossing it makes
with the rope passes under the rope. The rope therefore looks open on screen
and is closed for recognition.

\section{Decided knot choices}

Rappel stoppers: all options are taught. These are the double overhand, the
triple overhand (barrel), and the overhand on a bight.

Joining two ropes for rappel: all options are taught. These are the double
overhand and the flat overhand.

Figure-eight on a bight stopper: optional. The lesson notes that a stopper can
be added, and does not require it.

\section{Vocabulary}

The book uses these terms, and every chunk name starts with them.

\begin{description}
\item[Strand] A stretch of rope between two grab points, or between a grab point
and an end.
\item[Working end] The free end the player is tying with.
\item[Standing part] The part of the rope that is not being worked.
\item[Bight] A U-shaped loop of rope.
\item[Turn or wrap] One full pass of rope around an anchor object or another
part of the rope.
\item[Hitch] A knot that ties around an object.
\item[Bend] A knot that joins two ropes, or two ends of one rope.
\item[Loop] A knot that forms a closed loop in the rope.
\item[Friction hitch] A hitch that grips a rope under load and slides when
unloaded.
\item[Stopper] A knot at a rope end that keeps the rope from running through a
device.
\item[Closure] The way an open rope is closed for topology.
\item[Tension] A small pull that keeps the rope settled and responsive.
\end{description}
